Hi everyone,

I am working on developing a scale and am using the efa() function of lavaan for my current exploratory factor analysis (https://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/efa.html).
      fit <- efa(data = data[, items], nfactors = 15)
      sink("Title.txt")
      summary(fit, cutoff = 0.2, alpha.level = 0.05)

While interpreting the output, I noticed an irregularity in the significance of the correlations in my factor correlation matrix. For example, the correlation between f3 and f1 is -0.334 significant at p<.05, while the correlation between f3 and f2 is .521 but insignificant, see the picture attached. There is no missing data in the data set, meaning the differences in significance cannot be due to a difference in sample sizes.

Looking into this, I cannot find any documentation on how to interpret the significance of factor correlations. Further, cross-checking other packages supporting EFA analyses, no other package provides significance statistics for the factor correlation matrix.

Can someone help me understand how to interpret these significances or has a theory as to how these inconsistencies in the significance of correlations arise?

Any help or suggestions on where to further research this are highly appreciated! :)

Best, Sofie

