Background: I have tested a path model (Path.mod) which runs nicely on the set with complete responses.

RawResult.fit <-sem(Path.mod, Study1_OR)

I wanted to run the same model on a set of 5 imputed sets from SPSS. These were imported into dataframes via RStudio import dialogue.

Problem:

I tried two methods of creating a list of dataframes for use with RunMI.

(1)

MIMPS <- list(Study1_OR, Study1_IMP1, Study1_IMP2, Study1_IMP3, Study1_IMP4, Study1_IMP5)

verified:

> is.data.frame(Study1_IMP1) [1] TRUE

(repeated for each df)

> is.list(MIMPS) [1] TRUE

Trying to use runMI by:

Result.fit <- runMI(Path.mod, datalist=MIMPS, fun = "sem")

gave following error:

> Result.fit <- runMI(Path.mod, datalist=MIMPS, fun = "sem") Error in (function (model = NULL, dataList = NULL, dataFunction = NULL, : lavaan ERROR: (generated) data is not a data.frame (or a matrix)

(2)

Study1_IMP1$.imp <- 1
Study1_IMP2$.imp <- 2
Study1_IMP3$.imp <- 3
Study1_IMP4$.imp <- 4
Study1_IMP5$.imp <- 5
Study1_OR$.imp <-0
Imp_Long <-rbind(Study1_OR, Study1_IMP1, Study1_IMP2, Study1_IMP3, Study1_IMP4, Study1_IMP5)

Running this

Result.fit <- runMI(Path.mod, Imp_Long, m=5, fun = "sem")

gave a different error:

> Result.fit <- runMI(Path.mod, Imp_Long, m=5, fun = "sem") The resulting variance matrix was not invertible. Please check your data for highly collinear variables. The resulting variance matrix was not invertible. Please check your data for highly collinear variables. The resulting variance matrix was not invertible. Please check your data for highly collinear variables. The resulting variance matrix was not invertible. Please check your data for highly collinear variables. The resulting variance matrix was not invertible. Please check your data for highly collinear variables. Error in (function (model = NULL, dataList = NULL, dataFunction = NULL, : lavaan ERROR: (generated) data is not a data.frame (or a matrix) In addition: Warning message: In amcheck(x = x, m = m, idvars = numopts$idvars, priors = priors, : The variables (or variable with levels) IS_Total, HS_Total, RS_Total, Rumination_Total, MSCRQ_Pos_Total, MSCRQ_Neg_Total, RSES_Total, DASS_Depression, DASS_Anxiety, DASS_6I_Stress, External_Shame, Internal_Shame, MPoDTr_MetaAwareness, MPoDTr_DisIdentification, MPoDTr_NonReactivity are perfectly collinear with another variable in the data.

